    "recommendations": "Reducing the density and continuity of shrub cover and heavy brush in the immediate area surrounding the structure would meaningfully improve defensible space and reduce the opportunity for fire to spread directly to the building. Creating and maintaining separation between brush and the structure's exterior, including walls, vents, decks, and other attachments, may reduce direct fuel contact and improve conditions for suppression operations. Thinning and spacing vegetation between shrub masses and scattered trees could reduce vertical and horizontal fuel continuity throughout the property. Given that access to the property relies on a single vegetated route, maintaining cleared and navigable conditions along that driveway or access road is a priority, as it supports both firefighter ingress and occupant egress during an emergency. Keeping that corridor free of encroaching brush and low-hanging vegetation would support reliable two-way access and improve visibility along the route, contributing to long-term wildfire resilience and property preparedness."
